From 2dc5a5b16efdaa1431b93faa9dcbb34ace73338a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Harry Askham <harryaskham@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 24 Jun 2026 06:36:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] bd-05cda9: file-cache sync self-heals a stale state-sync.lock
 (the 4h fleet-wide wedge durable fix)

acquire_file_sync_lock opened the lock with create_new(true) and, on
AlreadyExists, errored UNCONDITIONALLY -- it never checked whether the recorded
holder PID was still alive. So a sync that hung (e.g. on a broken SSH key) and
was killed mid-hold left a STALE lock that blocked EVERY future sync forever:
the observed ~4h fleet-wide file-cache replication wedge (holder PID dead, lock
held 134min, every sync refused 'already exists') needed a manual ctrl clear.

Fix: on AlreadyExists, self-heal a STALE lock. The lock records 'pid=<N> ts=...';
file_sync_lock_is_stale reads it and, if the holder PID is not alive
(kill(pid, 0) -> ESRCH), clears the lock and retries the create once. Only a
genuinely-live holder still refuses. Conservative throughout: a live PID is
never stale, EPERM (exists-but-not-ours) counts as alive, and an unparseable
lock is only stale past a 900s floor -- so a racing sync's fresh lock is never
cleared. Mirrors the daemon's git index.lock cleanup + the kill(pid,0) liveness
pattern used elsewhere in the workspace.

Refactors the create+stamp into try_create_file_sync_lock (reused for the
post-clear retry). + unit test file_sync_lock_stale_self_heal_bd_05cda9
(dead-holder stale, live-holder kept, young-unparseable kept). caco-cli only.

Follow-up (noted in bead): also bound the sync's git ops with a process-group
timeout so a hang is killed + the lock RAII-released rather than lingering.
